# Core-suite tests whose output differs with dmbtree indexes (test/run.sh
# core-dm), on top of test/core/expected_failures.  Same format.  In core-dm
# every index CREATE INDEX or a PRIMARY KEY/UNIQUE constraint makes on an
# iheap table is a dmbtree index, unique ones included (test/core/core-dm.conf).
#
# Recurring reasons:
#   name      the output names access method dmbtree (\d, pg_indexes,
#             pg_index.indclass, pg_depend: dmbtree's operator classes are
#             not pinned) where it names btree
#   usingidx  ADD CONSTRAINT ... USING INDEX takes btree indexes only
#             (parse_utilcmd.c: "index ... is not a btree"); the default
#             iheap.unique_index_am = btree avoids it
#   arbiter   ON CONFLICT naming an operator class resolves the class for
#             btree (parse_clause.c), so no dmbtree unique index matches
#   fk        foreign key checks against a dmbtree key take the SPI path
#             (core's fast path is btree-only), so errors carry a CONTEXT line
#   opclass   the test creates a btree operator class after the extension:
#             dmbtree has no copy, and the index stays btree with a NOTICE
#   order     an UPDATE of an indexed column is in place: the row keeps its
#             place, where heap appends the new version
#   family    the test adds operators to a built-in btree family after the
#             extension copied it: dmbtree's copy lacks them, so the planner
#             keeps to dmbtree's own operators for those indexes (as btree,
#             they would be index conditions the executor cannot look up in
#             the index's family)

# --- regress: only in core-dm
regress/cluster            order (updates of the clustering column); name
regress/collate            name
regress/compression        name
regress/create_table       name
regress/create_table_like  name
regress/domain             name
regress/equivclass         family (integer_ops gains int8alias operators)
regress/event_trigger      usingidx: create_index's ADD PRIMARY KEY USING INDEX fails, which leaves concur_reindex_tab behind
regress/generated_virtual  name
regress/index_including    name
regress/indexing           name
regress/matview            name
regress/publication        usingidx
regress/replica_identity   name
regress/tablespace         name
regress/typed_table        name
regress/without_overlaps   usingidx

# --- regress: also in expected_failures, with these differences on top
regress/alter_table        usingidx
regress/constraints        usingidx
regress/create_index       usingidx; name
regress/foreign_key        fk; opclass
regress/insert_conflict    arbiter; a plan (an EXISTS subquery on the small table takes a seq scan)
regress/rules              arbiter

# --- isolation: only in core-dm
isolation/insert-conflict-specconflict  the index is on an expression, which dmbtree takes in-place updates through (§21 D39): a conflict probe (SnapshotDirty) checks the row's key against the unmarked entry it found (§11.5.4), so the expression's function runs once more per probe and repeats its NOTICEs
